1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,849
[MUSIC]

2
00:00:15,815 --> 00:00:19,740
There are two general ways that
fragments get added to activities.

3
00:00:19,740 --> 00:00:23,520
First, they can be statically
added to the activity.

4
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:27,930
For example, by putting them
into the activity's layout file,

5
00:00:27,930 --> 00:00:31,815
which is then used in
a call to setContentView.

6
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:39,220
Alternatively, you can add fragments
problematically using the FragmentManager.

7
00:00:39,220 --> 00:00:41,080
However they get added though,

8
00:00:41,080 --> 00:00:46,360
once they're added,
Android will make a call to onCreateView.

9
00:00:46,360 --> 00:00:51,290
And within onCreateView,
the fragment can use its own XML layout,

10
00:00:51,290 --> 00:00:56,640
similar to what activities do
when they call setContentView, or

11
00:00:56,640 --> 00:01:00,010
they can programmatically
build their user interfaces.

12
00:01:00,010 --> 00:01:03,605
And again, however it's done,
once the user interface view is built,

13
00:01:03,605 --> 00:01:10,470
onCreateView must return the view that's
at the root of its user interface layout.

14
00:01:10,470 --> 00:01:14,650
And that view will eventually be
given to the hosting activity and

15
00:01:14,650 --> 00:01:17,530
added to the hosting
activity's user interface.

16
00:01:18,810 --> 00:01:23,280
So, let's look back at
the FragmentStaticLayout application and

17
00:01:23,280 --> 00:01:25,390
see how it defines its layout.

18
00:01:27,660 --> 00:01:28,660
Here's the application again.

19
00:01:28,660 --> 00:01:35,089
And as you remember, there's a panel
on the left showing the play titles and

20
00:01:35,089 --> 00:01:39,019
there's a panel on
the right showing quotes.

21
00:01:39,019 --> 00:01:43,227
And each of these panels is
implemented by a different fragment,

22
00:01:43,227 --> 00:01:47,683
one is called TitleFragment and
the other is called QuoteFragment.

23
00:01:47,683 --> 00:01:53,550
And this application's main activity
is called QuoteViewerActivity.

24
00:01:53,550 --> 00:01:57,670
Let's open up the QuoteViewerActivity
file and see how it handles or

25
00:01:57,670 --> 00:01:59,750
creates its layout.

26
00:01:59,750 --> 00:02:02,720
Now first,
you see that in the onCreate method,

27
00:02:02,720 --> 00:02:09,970
there's a call to setContentView with
the parameter value R.layout.main.

28
00:02:09,970 --> 00:02:17,137
So let's look in the res/layout
directory for the file called main.xml.

29
00:02:17,137 --> 00:02:19,390
And here it is and let me open it up.

30
00:02:20,990 --> 00:02:24,680
As you can see, the entire layout
is comprised of something called

31
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:30,240
a LinearLayout and
that LinearLayout contains two fragments.

32
00:02:31,380 --> 00:02:36,990
And inside those Fragment tags,
there's an attribute called class.

33
00:02:36,990 --> 00:02:39,870
And the value of this
attribute is the name of

34
00:02:39,870 --> 00:02:43,300
the class that implements that fragment.

35
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:48,822
So in this case, one fragment is
implemented by the TitleFragment class and

36
00:02:48,822 --> 00:02:52,890
the other is implemented by
the QuotesFragment class.

37
00:02:54,320 --> 00:02:55,320
Anyway, that's enough for now.

38
00:02:55,320 --> 00:02:58,930
We'll talk more about layouts
when we get to user interfaces.

39
00:02:58,930 --> 00:03:02,110
In any event, when this XML file is read,

40
00:03:02,110 --> 00:03:06,950
Android will understand that it needs
to create these two fragments and

41
00:03:06,950 --> 00:03:10,870
that it needs to install them
in the QuoteViewer activity.

42
00:03:10,870 --> 00:03:15,490
And this will start the chain of lifecycle
calls that we talked about earlier.

43
00:03:15,490 --> 00:03:19,692
And as I said, one of those calls
will be a call to onCreate view,

44
00:03:19,692 --> 00:03:25,640
in which the fragment is responsible for
creating its user interface layout.

45
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:27,950
Let's take a look at one of those layouts,

46
00:03:27,950 --> 00:03:33,610
the one in QuoteFragment,
to see how it creates its user interface.

47
00:03:33,610 --> 00:03:40,860
Now here's the QuoteFragment class and
down here is its onCreateView method.

48
00:03:40,860 --> 00:03:46,290
This method calls the inflate
method of the LayoutInflater class,

49
00:03:46,290 --> 00:03:49,630
passing in a layout file as a parameter,
and

50
00:03:49,630 --> 00:03:54,458
the effect of this is pretty similar
to what happens in setContentView.

51
00:03:54,458 --> 00:03:58,240
Specifically, an XML file is read in and

52
00:03:58,240 --> 00:04:03,669
converted into Java objects corresponding
to some user interface views.

53
00:04:05,710 --> 00:04:10,450
You can also add fragments to an activity
without hardcoding the fragments into

54
00:04:10,450 --> 00:04:12,800
the activity's layout file.

55
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:18,290
And to do that, while the activity is
running, you have to do four things.

56
00:04:18,290 --> 00:04:21,970
One, get a reference to
the FragmentManager.

57
00:04:21,970 --> 00:04:25,450
Two, begin a FragmentTransaction.

58
00:04:25,450 --> 00:04:28,460
Three, add a Fragment to the activity.

59
00:04:29,550 --> 00:04:33,340
And four, commit the FragmentTransaction.

60
00:04:33,340 --> 00:04:39,664
Now to see this in action, let's take
the FragmentStaticLayout application and

61
00:04:39,664 --> 00:04:45,162
change it a bit so that the fragments
are added programmatically rather

62
00:04:45,162 --> 00:04:50,586
than being added as part,
as part of the QuoteViewer's layout file.

63
00:04:50,586 --> 00:04:52,280
Now here we are back in the IDE.

64
00:04:54,010 --> 00:04:59,610
I've created a new application called
FragmentProgrammaticLayout and

65
00:04:59,610 --> 00:05:02,980
it's supposed to look exactly
like FragmentStaticLayout.

66
00:05:02,980 --> 00:05:07,660
The only difference is how the fragments
get added to the main activity.

67
00:05:07,660 --> 00:05:11,380
Let's open up the QuoteViewerActivity and
see how it handles its layout.

68
00:05:12,660 --> 00:05:14,160
Now here in onCreate,

69
00:05:14,160 --> 00:05:20,500
there's a call to setContentView with
a parameter value R.layout.main.

70
00:05:20,500 --> 00:05:25,430
So let's look in the res/layout
directory for that main.xml file.

71
00:05:26,850 --> 00:05:28,510
Now here it is.

72
00:05:28,510 --> 00:05:29,260
Let me open it up.

73
00:05:31,320 --> 00:05:38,480
Now as you can see, the entire layout is
again a LinearLayout with two subviews.

74
00:05:38,480 --> 00:05:42,500
But instead of it being,
the subviews being fragments, this time,

75
00:05:42,500 --> 00:05:44,649
the subviews are frame layouts.

76
00:05:45,780 --> 00:05:51,180
The frame layout is to essentially reserve
some space in the user interface and

77
00:05:51,180 --> 00:05:55,970
we'll fill the space in later when we
add the fragments to this activity.

78
00:05:57,200 --> 00:06:00,685
Now, let's go back to the onCreate
method of QuoteViewerActivity.

79
00:06:01,890 --> 00:06:06,158
Now we'll walk through the steps of
adding the fragments into the user inter,

80
00:06:06,158 --> 00:06:07,730
into the activity.

81
00:06:07,730 --> 00:06:10,610
And just like I said earlier,
there are four steps.

82
00:06:10,610 --> 00:06:13,385
First, we get a reference
to the FragmentManager.

83
00:06:14,510 --> 00:06:19,840
Then we call the beginTransaction
method on the FragmentManager.

84
00:06:19,840 --> 00:06:22,175
And that returns a FragmentTransaction.

85
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:29,550
Next, we call the add method of the
FragmentTransaction, passing in an ID for

86
00:06:29,550 --> 00:06:35,640
the frame layout and passing in
the fragment that that view will hold.

87
00:06:36,690 --> 00:06:40,095
And we'll do that for both the
TitleFragment and for the QuoteFragment.

88
00:06:41,780 --> 00:06:45,365
And finally, we'll call the commit
method on the FragmentTransaction.

89
00:06:47,800 --> 00:06:50,470
Now, let's run the code and make sure
that we haven't goofed anything up.

90
00:06:52,910 --> 00:06:58,880
And here's the FragmentProgrammaticLayout
application running on an emulated tablet.

91
00:06:58,880 --> 00:07:00,570
And it seems to operate as promised.

92
00:07:02,330 --> 00:07:06,940
So, now we know how to add fragments
to an activity programmatically.

93
00:07:06,940 --> 00:07:08,800
But in our example,

94
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:12,430
it didn't really make that much
difference in the sense that even though

95
00:07:12,430 --> 00:07:17,320
we added the fragments programmatically,
the layout itself was static.

96
00:07:17,320 --> 00:07:21,650
There are always exactly two panels and
that never changes.

97
00:07:21,650 --> 00:07:25,940
Now, one of the nice things about being
able to add fragments on the fly,

98
00:07:25,940 --> 00:07:29,690
however, is that it allows you
to dynamically change the user

99
00:07:29,690 --> 00:07:32,360
interface while the program is running.

100
00:07:32,360 --> 00:07:33,680
And if you do this right,

101
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:37,950
it allows you to make good use
of your precious screen space.

102
00:07:37,950 --> 00:07:42,000
So, let's take a look at a simple example
that gives you a little flavor of

103
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:44,250
this dynamic user interfaces.

104
00:07:45,720 --> 00:07:50,140
I'll launch an application called
FragmentDynamicLayout, which, again,

105
00:07:50,140 --> 00:07:54,630
is similar to the previous examples,
but sometimes it

106
00:07:54,630 --> 00:07:59,900
will show a single fragment and
sometimes it will show multiple fragments.

107
00:07:59,900 --> 00:08:02,420
So here the application,
we've just started it up and

108
00:08:02,420 --> 00:08:05,140
it's showing a single fragment.

109
00:08:05,140 --> 00:08:08,140
It's showing the TitleFragment and
nothing else.

110
00:08:09,140 --> 00:08:13,722
And in fact, you can see here that I've
changed the title of Hamlet to make it

111
00:08:13,722 --> 00:08:14,868
a little longer and

112
00:08:14,868 --> 00:08:18,822
you see that it takes up much more
of the screen space than before.

113
00:08:18,822 --> 00:08:22,955
And in addition, if I click on that title,
you can see that the item is

114
00:08:22,955 --> 00:08:26,750
indeed taking up the whole,
the entire screen horizontally.

115
00:08:28,660 --> 00:08:31,600
Now that I finished clicking
on the title though,

116
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:34,150
you can see the familiar
quote from Hamlet.

117
00:08:34,150 --> 00:08:38,120
And at this point, the application
is displaying two fragments.

118
00:08:39,150 --> 00:08:41,490
If I now hit the Back button,

119
00:08:41,490 --> 00:08:44,820
you see that we've gone back to
