120
00:08:46,080 --> 00:08:47,360
So let's take a look at the source code.

121
00:08:49,030 --> 00:08:51,550
Now here I am back in the IDE.

122
00:08:51,550 --> 00:08:55,400
And I'll open up
the FragmentDynamicLayout application.

123
00:08:55,400 --> 00:08:59,593
And we'll take a look at
the QuoteViewerActivity file.

124
00:08:59,593 --> 00:09:02,667
Now first,
here's the call to setContentView,

125
00:09:02,667 --> 00:09:05,120
passing in the main.xml file.

126
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:07,530
And this is pretty similar
to what we saw before.

127
00:09:09,100 --> 00:09:14,020
Next, we'll start the process of
adding a fragment to this activity.

128
00:09:14,020 --> 00:09:17,770
This time, however,
we'll only add the TitleFragment.

129
00:09:17,770 --> 00:09:21,040
In fact, we'll only ever add

130
00:09:21,040 --> 00:09:24,660
the quote fragment if the user
indeed clicks on a title.

131
00:09:25,700 --> 00:09:29,430
Now, supposing the user
does click on the title.

132
00:09:29,430 --> 00:09:32,760
The onList selection method
will then be called.

133
00:09:34,060 --> 00:09:35,240
Now let's take a look at that method.

134
00:09:36,850 --> 00:09:40,680
First, we'll test to see if
the quote fragment has already been

135
00:09:40,680 --> 00:09:41,740
added to the layout.

136
00:09:43,710 --> 00:09:48,175
And if it hasn't, then we'll add it now
by starting another FragmentTransaction.

137
00:09:49,690 --> 00:09:51,440
So, two things are new here.

138
00:09:51,440 --> 00:09:55,680
First, we're going to add this
transaction to the task back stack.

139
00:09:56,740 --> 00:09:59,570
And that way, when the user
hits the Back button, we'll go

140
00:09:59,570 --> 00:10:04,320
back to the one fragment layout that we
had before it was, before it was added.

141
00:10:04,320 --> 00:10:06,915
And we have to do this because,

142
00:10:06,915 --> 00:10:11,420
by default, fragment changes
are not tracked by the back stack.

143
00:10:12,730 --> 00:10:16,865
The second new thing is that at the end,
I've added a call to

144
00:10:16,865 --> 00:10:21,395
FragmentManager.executePendingTransacti-
ons.

145
00:10:22,530 --> 00:10:26,530
And this forces the transaction
to be executed immediately,

146
00:10:26,530 --> 00:10:31,400
rather than at some later time when
the system finds it convenient to do so.

147
00:10:31,400 --> 00:10:35,616
And again, I have to do this because
otherwise, Android might, it might take,

148
00:10:35,616 --> 00:10:39,281
you know, god knows how long before
it actually updates the display.

149
00:10:42,259 --> 00:10:47,891
Now, if you remember back to our lesson
on the Activity class, I talked about

150
00:10:47,891 --> 00:10:53,267
how activities can handle configuration
changes manually using methods

151
00:10:53,267 --> 00:10:59,703
such as onRetainNonConfigurationInstance
and getLastNonConfigurationInstance.

152
00:10:59,703 --> 00:11:04,759
And at that time, I mentioned that those
methods were deprecated in favor of

153
00:11:04,759 --> 00:11:07,980
other methods in the Fragment class.

154
00:11:07,980 --> 00:11:09,790
So let's talk about those methods now.

155
00:11:09,790 --> 00:11:15,870
Now, if you're using a fragment and
the device's configuration changes,

156
00:11:15,870 --> 00:11:21,440
then Android, by default, will kill
the hosting activity and recreate it,

157
00:11:21,440 --> 00:11:23,740
just like we talked about before.

158
00:11:23,740 --> 00:11:29,030
However, if you call the setRetainInstance
method on the fragment,

159
00:11:29,030 --> 00:11:35,620
passing in the value true as a parameter,
then when configuration changes occur,

160
00:11:35,620 --> 00:11:41,720
Android will kill the activity, but not
destroy the fragments that it's hosting.

161
00:11:41,720 --> 00:11:44,550
Instead, it will save
that fragment state and

162
00:11:44,550 --> 00:11:47,050
it will detach the fragment
from the activity.

163
00:11:48,900 --> 00:11:52,290
And in addition,
it won't destroy that fragment and so,

164
00:11:52,290 --> 00:11:56,810
it won't recreate it later when
the hosting activity is recreated.

165
00:11:56,810 --> 00:12:02,730
And as a result, the fragment will not
receive calls to its onDestroy method nor

166
00:12:02,730 --> 00:12:04,037
to its onCreate method.

167
00:12:05,270 --> 00:12:05,850
Let's take a look at

168
00:12:05,850 --> 00:12:08,980
another example application that
demonstrates this behavior.

169
00:12:10,300 --> 00:12:15,040
This application is called
FragmentStaticConfigLayout and

170
00:12:15,040 --> 00:12:18,099
its functionality is the same
as the previous examples.

171
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:21,350
The difference here though,

172
00:12:21,350 --> 00:12:24,310
is that I've added some code to
handle configuration changes.

173
00:12:25,590 --> 00:12:27,680
Now, when the device is in landscape mode,

174
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:31,910
the layout is basically what we've seen so
far.

175
00:12:31,910 --> 00:12:39,140
Both fragments use the large font of 32sp,
or scale-independent pixels.

176
00:12:39,140 --> 00:12:42,835
The TitleFragment takes about
a third of the horizontal space,

177
00:12:42,835 --> 00:12:47,750
while the QuoteFragment takes
the remaining two-thirds of the space.

178
00:12:47,750 --> 00:12:52,910
And if a title is too long to fit on
a single line in the TitleFragment,

179
00:12:52,910 --> 00:12:55,260
that text will simply wrap
around to a second line.

180
00:12:56,860 --> 00:13:00,660
When the device is in portrait mode,
however, the layout changes a bit.

181
00:13:01,660 --> 00:13:06,000
Both fragments use a smaller 24sp font and

182
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:10,010
the TitleFragment takes up only
a fourth of the horizontal space,

183
00:13:10,010 --> 00:13:13,530
while the QuoteFragment takes up
the remaining three-quarters of the space.

184
00:13:14,640 --> 00:13:18,569
And if a title is too long to fit on
a single line in the TitleFragment,

185
00:13:19,820 --> 00:13:23,680
then that title will still be
limited to a single line and

186
00:13:23,680 --> 00:13:28,140
we'll just replace some of
the text with ellipses.

187
00:13:28,140 --> 00:13:29,797
Let's see that application in action.

188
00:13:33,524 --> 00:13:37,500
I'll start the application with
the device in landscape mode.

189
00:13:37,500 --> 00:13:39,460
I'll select one of
the title to show a quote.

190
00:13:40,980 --> 00:13:45,345
And as I said, the TitleFragment and
QuoteFragment will use larger fonts.

191
00:13:45,345 --> 00:13:51,410
They're dividing up the screen in a
roughly one-fourth to three-fourths ratio.

192
00:13:51,410 --> 00:13:53,690
And in the TitleFragment,

193
00:13:53,690 --> 00:13:56,890
it's allowing the title of Hamlet
to spill over onto a second line.

194
00:13:58,330 --> 00:13:59,840
Now, let's rotate the device.

195
00:14:01,090 --> 00:14:04,690
Now, one thing that you notice is that
even though Android has killed and

196
00:14:04,690 --> 00:14:09,010
restarted the QuoteViewerActivity,
the title that I

197
00:14:09,010 --> 00:14:12,510
previously checked is still checked, and

198
00:14:12,510 --> 00:14:19,450
that's because I made sure to call
setRetainInstance true in both fragments.

199
00:14:19,450 --> 00:14:21,400
So, information, for

200
00:14:21,400 --> 00:14:25,210
example, information about which
item was checked, has been retained.

201
00:14:25,210 --> 00:14:28,710
And we'll see how that's
done in just a second.

202
00:14:28,710 --> 00:14:34,260
You'll also notice that now that we're in
portrait mode, the layout has changed.

203
00:14:34,260 --> 00:14:38,960
In fact, the fonts are smaller now and
instead of that Hamlet title spanning two

204
00:14:38,960 --> 00:14:43,060
lines, the end of the title has
been replaced with ellipsis.

205
00:14:44,260 --> 00:14:45,780
So, let's go see how this
look works in the code.

206
00:14:47,810 --> 00:14:52,350
Now here, I've opened
the FragmentStaticConfigLayout application

207
00:14:52,350 --> 00:14:52,890
in the IDE.

208
00:14:54,270 --> 00:14:56,960
Next, I'll open up
the TitleFragment.java file.

209
00:14:58,660 --> 00:15:01,390
And as you can see,
this code is mostly the same as what we

210
00:15:01,390 --> 00:15:06,400
saw in the other example applications,
but there are at least two differences.

211
00:15:06,400 --> 00:15:08,090
Let's look at those.

212
00:15:08,090 --> 00:15:11,720
The first difference is
in the onCreate method.

213
00:15:11,720 --> 00:15:14,620
I've added a call to setRetainInstance,
true.

214
00:15:16,940 --> 00:15:21,160
And again, this means that when
configuration changes occur,

215
00:15:21,160 --> 00:15:22,940
Android will not kill this fragment.

216
00:15:22,940 --> 00:15:27,630
The second difference is in
the onActivityCreated method.

217
00:15:27,630 --> 00:15:31,620
And right here at the end,
I've added some code that checks to

218
00:15:31,620 --> 00:15:37,400
see whether m current index
is not equal to minus 1.

219
00:15:37,400 --> 00:15:42,390
And if that's true, it means that the user
has previously selected a title and so,

220
00:15:42,390 --> 00:15:46,460
this call to onActivityCreated
is probably occurring because of

221
00:15:46,460 --> 00:15:48,480
a configuration change.

222
00:15:48,480 --> 00:15:52,219
So in this case, we want to make sure
that that title remains checked.

223
00:15:53,310 --> 00:15:57,610
Now, now I made similar changes to
the QuoteFragment class as well.

